<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Adolf Schulten</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1870">1870</a>–<a href="/years/1960">1960</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Adolf Schulten was a German historian and archaeologist of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. From 1905 he excavated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/numantia">Numantia</a>, uncovering the Celtiberian town and the ring of camps and walls built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-aemilianus">Scipio Aemilianus</a> for the siege of 134–133 BCE, which he published in a multi-volume study. He also searched for the legendary city of Tartessos and wrote widely on the Roman conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>.</p></div>
